      "text": "Collections should be generated\nfrom the metadata. There will\nbe either collections from our\ncontrolled vocabulary subject or\ncollections from thematic\ncollections we develop. \n\nWe use Dublin Core. There will\nbe a subject:collection tag and\nthese would be the name of the\ncollection (as is here). \n\nIn the metadata, editions tagged\nappropriately will form the\neditions that make up the\ncollection. One caveat -- some\ncollections will not be\ngenerated from the subject tag.\nFor others, that are part of a\nbespoke collection, typically\nfrom an institution or project,\nwe will use 'IsPartOf: collection.\n\nAlso -- editions can appear in\none or more collections. \n\nPS from Susan -- now that I\nwrote all that, I think all\ncollections should be generated\nfrom a IsPartOf tag that will be\neditorially assigned ",
      "fontSize": 16,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Collections should be generated from the metadata. There will be either collections from our controlled vocabulary subject or collections from thematic collections we develop. \n\nWe use Dublin Core. There will be a subject:collection tag and these would be the name of the collection (as is here). \n\nIn the metadata, editions tagged appropriately will form the editions that make up the collection. One caveat -- some collections will not be generated from the subject tag. For others, that are part of a bespoke collection, typically from an institution or project, we will use 'IsPartOf: collection. \n\nAlso -- editions can appear in one or more collections. \n\nPS from Susan -- now that I wrote all that, I think all collections should be generated from a IsPartOf tag that will be editorially assigned ",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
